    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=3 y^{2} x^{2}+2 y x,$ evaluate $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}$ at the point $(1,1).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3 y^{2} x^{2}+2 y x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3 y^{2} x^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2 y x) \\\\  & =3 x^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y^{2})+2 x\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y) \\\\  & =3 x^{2}\\cdot (2 y)+2 x\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =6 y x^{2}+2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}{|}_{(1,1)} & =6 (1) (1)^{2}+2 (1) \\\\  & =6+(2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
